INT. BOOKBINDER'S WORKSHOP - LISBON - EVENING

A narrow stone room under a vaulted ceiling. Presses, bone folders, spools of waxed linen. Every surface is stacked with half-finished volumes, their spines bare as ribs. A single window frames the red roofs of the Alfama and a tram rattling past.

SOLEDAD IBARRA, 30s, in a leather apron, sits at the sewing frame with a needle in her hand, a loose braid down her back. Ink stains her fingertips. She is stitching a signature with practiced ease.

ARCHIE MENDELSOHN, 30s, a tall American in a rain-spotted jacket, stands in the doorway holding a battered book against his chest like a rescued animal.

SOLEDAD
You're late.

ARCHIE
I got lost.

SOLEDAD
You always get lost.

ARCHIE
It's a very confusing city.

SOLEDAD
It's a city with seven hills and one river. Even the pigeons find their way.

ARCHIE
The pigeons live here.

SOLEDAD
So do I. And I'm never late.

ARCHIE
You're never anywhere else.

She smiles despite herself, not looking up. He crosses to the workbench and sets the book down in front of her. Gently, as if it might bruise.

ARCHIE (CONT'D)
That's the one.

SOLEDAD
(inspecting)
Oh, Archie. What did you do to it?

ARCHIE
I dropped it in a canal.

SOLEDAD
In Venice.

ARCHIE
In Venice.

SOLEDAD
On purpose?

ARCHIE
It jumped. I'm telling you, it jumped.

SOLEDAD
Books don't jump.

ARCHIE
This one has a lot of feelings.

She opens the cover. The pages are warped and rippled, the ink bled into soft blue clouds. She runs a thumb along the gutter and lets out a slow breath.

SOLEDAD
Whose is it?

ARCHIE
My grandfather's. His diary.

SOLEDAD
Then why are you giving it to me?

ARCHIE
Because you're the best in Europe.

SOLEDAD
Flattery.

ARCHIE
Fact.

SOLEDAD
It will take weeks.

ARCHIE
I have weeks.

SOLEDAD
You have a plane ticket.

ARCHIE
I could lose the ticket.

SOLEDAD
You could.

ARCHIE
I could lose the whole plane.

SOLEDAD
(smiling)
Archie.

ARCHIE
Soledad.

The workshop door bangs open. AUNT VELMA, 70s, a small woman in a patterned housecoat and bright orange slippers, sweeps in with a covered dish and a gust of cold air.

AUNT VELMA
Soledad! You haven't eaten. I brought cod.

SOLEDAD
Tia, I'm working.

AUNT VELMA
(to Archie)
And who is this?

ARCHIE
Archie Mendelsohn, ma'am. I'm a client.

AUNT VELMA
A client who brings flowers?

He looks down. He is, in fact, holding a very small bunch of wilted daisies in his left hand, crushed against the book. He lifts them with a helpless shrug.

ARCHIE
They were fresh this morning.

AUNT VELMA
(to Soledad, delighted)
He is a client who brings flowers.

SOLEDAD
Tia.

AUNT VELMA
I will leave the cod.

She sets the dish on the nearest stack of paper, pats Archie's cheek once, and sweeps out. The door slams. The tram rattles by. Soledad and Archie look at each other across the ruined book.

ARCHIE
She likes me.

SOLEDAD
She likes everybody. She feeds the pigeons.

ARCHIE
Do you?

SOLEDAD
Do I what?

ARCHIE
Like me.

She picks up the daisies. Smooths one battered petal between thumb and finger.

SOLEDAD
Come back on Tuesday. I will tell you what the book needs.

ARCHIE
And the other thing?

SOLEDAD
(not looking up)
The other thing takes longer.
